#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""cc-scope — the ask ledger: every ask the owner makes is written down, and 'done' is measured against it.

The board tracks what the BOX decided to do; this is the other half, one row per ask, opened when it is made
and closed only against evidence. A compound ask ("do X, and also Y") gets closed on X, and a standing rule
said once in a thread is forgotten by the next session — the ledger is what stops both. Rows are written by
hand: `add` for one ask, `import` for a seed file.

Ledger: ~/.cc/scope/<repo>.json  (sidecar like the board — never in git, so no merge noise).

An ask moves  open → claimed → merged → delivered → verified,  or leaves the queue as  declined.

MERGED IS NOT DELIVERED. A merge puts code on the default branch; it does not put anything on the owner's
screen. The daemon still runs the old code until it is restarted, the file is not linked until install.sh
runs, the tab does not carry the block until it is rendered. Rows were closed on "merged as PR #N" and the
owner himself found the chart was not there — so a PR number is no longer evidence of delivery, and no
sentence is. A row names its OWN verification, `check`: a shell command whose EXIT CODE decides. `deliver`
RUNS it. Exit 0 and the row is delivered, because something observable was true at that moment. Anything
else and the row stays exactly where it was.

A ROW WITH NO CHECK IS NOT DONE, IT IS UNVERIFIED. That is the honest answer and it is the point: not every
ask has a mechanical check, and where one genuinely cannot exist the row stays on the queue and says so
(`unverified`) rather than quietly counting as done. A person may still close it with `verify --evidence`,
which is them putting their own name on it — but that is a person's word, in a terminal, and it is refused
outright while the row names a check that has not passed. Nothing automated can reach `verified`.

DECLINED IS A DECISION, NOT A DROP: a session may judge that something should not be built, and that is a
legitimate answer — but it must say why, in its own words, and the reason stays in the ledger next to the ask.
`--why` is therefore required. An omission leaves an open item; a decision leaves a reason; the two can never
be mistaken for each other, which is the whole point of writing the asks down.

A CHECK THAT CANNOT RUN IS NOT A CHECK THAT FAILED. Both are rc≠0 and they mean opposite things, so they are
told apart: rc 126/127 is "no answer", it exits 3, it says so on every line you read, and it never overwrites
a check that once passed. The other half is that a check should outlive its track — `check-cmd --script`
copies the script in beside the ledger, and a check that runs a script out of a directory which dies with its
track (a track's state dir, its worktree, /tmp) is refused.

A CHECK THAT READS WHAT THE TREE NO LONGER HAS is no answer either, whatever rc it came back with. The check is
frozen at the ask; the tree moves. a218's check read ~/.cc/state/steward/runs.jsonl, the ledger cc-steward
kept; #483 deleted cc-steward and the row read "merged, NOT delivered" for ever, and a seat read "steward last
ran 23 h ago" off it and spent turns probing watchers that were fine (2026-09-16). So a check that FAILS is read
once more before it is called a failure: every path of the repo's checkout it names, every bare `cc-<name>`, and
every ~/.cc/state/<name>/ (the ledger cc-<name> keeps) is asked of git on the base branch — gone, and once
deleted, means the check measures nothing. That is exit 3, naming the path and the deleting commit, on every
line you read. A check that PASSES is never second-guessed: something observable was true.

A CHECK IS NOT REPLACED IN SILENCE. check-cmd will not replace a row's check without `--replace "<why>"`,
prints the one that is there when it refuses, and keeps the reason in the row's notes. Every script under
checks/ a check runs is fingerprinted when it is registered: `deliver` refuses (exit 3) to run one edited
since, because a check quietly written over by another seat passed an ask on a fixture (a342, 2026-09-12).

  cc-scope add <repo> "<ask>" [--src REF] [--track T] [--done-when "..."] [--check "<cmd>"] [--who W] [--at ISO]
  cc-scope list [<repo>] [--all] [--status S] [--track T] [--json]   unsettled asks (all repos if none named)
  cc-scope show <repo> <id>                                          one ask in full: parts, repeats, notes
  cc-scope claim <repo> <id> [--track T]                             a session/track has taken it on
  cc-scope check-cmd <repo> <id> "<command>" | --script <file>       the row's own verification; exit 0 = true
            [--replace "<why>"]                                      required when the row already has one
  cc-scope merged <repo> <id> --evidence "<PR or commit>"            it landed. NOT delivered — still work
  cc-scope deliver <repo> <id>                                       RUNS the check; exit 0 → delivered. A MERGED row whose
                                                                     check fails is written to the failures ledger, once per
                                                                     ask and merge (delivery/merged-not-delivered, via cc-green red)
  cc-scope verify <repo> <id> [--evidence "…"]                       a PERSON measured it against the ask
  cc-scope unverified [<repo>]                                       landed with nothing observed; exit 1 if any
  cc-scope decline <repo> <id> --why "<why it should not be built>"  a decision, kept in the ledger
  cc-scope track|note <repo> <id> "<text>"                           link an ask to a track / add a note
  cc-scope check <repo> [--track T]                                  exit 1 while anything is unsettled
  cc-scope digest [<repo>] · count [<repo>] · import <repo> <file> · selfcheck
  cc-scope hook                                                      a no-op kept for a settings file that still names it

Exit, for the commands a caller acts on: 0 did it · 1 the check ran and FAILED · 2 the row names no check ·
3 the check COULD NOT RUN, or was not run because its script changed since it was registered, so nothing was
measured either way.
"""
import sys, os, re, json, time, fcntl, hashlib, shutil, shlex, subprocess

HOME = os.path.expanduser("~")
SCOPE = os.environ.get("CC_SCOPE_DIR", os.path.join(HOME, ".cc", "scope"))
STATES = ["open", "claimed", "merged", "delivered", "verified", "declined"]
# Off the queue. `merged` is deliberately NOT here — that is the whole fix: code on the default branch is
# still work until something observes it. Declined is settled and STAYS VISIBLE — see the header.
SETTLED = ("delivered", "verified", "declined")
DONE = ("delivered", "verified")     # it actually happened, by a check or by a person's own word
MARK = {"open": "·", "claimed": "▸", "merged": "→", "delivered": "✓", "verified": "✓", "declined": "⊘"}
EXAMPLES = "systemctl --user is-active <unit> · test bin/x -nt <the merge> · a grep of what the tab renders"
CHECK_TIMEOUT = 300  # a verification is meant to be CHEAP: one grep, one systemctl is-active, one curl
EXCERPT = 800          # what a row keeps of a long ask: a whole Slack thread is not a ledger line; the rest is in full_path()
# bash's own two codes for "there was nothing to run": 127 not found, 126 found but not executable. A check
# that comes back either of these did not answer the question — see CANNOT RUN in the header.
CANNOT_RUN = (126, 127)
CHECK_TIMED_OUT = 124  # timeout(1)'s code. A check that HUNG did run, and it is a real failure, not silence.
CHECKS = "checks"      # ~/.cc/scope/checks/ — where a check script lives so it outlives the track that wrote it
# A path that dies with the track that made it: a track's own state dir (~/.cc/state/<repo>/<track>/…), a
# worktree, /tmp. Matched only where the command RUNS the thing, so `test ! -d ~/.cc/state/x/y` — a check
# that the janitor cleaned up — is still allowed.
_HOMEISH = r"(?:~|\$HOME|\$\{HOME\}|/home/[^/\s]+|%s)" % re.escape(HOME)
DOOMED = re.compile(r"(?:(?:^|[;&|(])|(?<![\w./-])(?:bash|sh|source|\.)(?=[ \t]))[ \t]*"
                    r"(" + _HOMEISH + r"/\.cc/(?:state/[^/\s]+/[^/\s]+|worktrees)/\S+|/tmp/\S+)")


def now(): return time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime())
def path(repo): return os.path.join(SCOPE, repo + ".json")
def full_path(repo, aid): return os.path.join(SCOPE, repo + ".asks", aid + ".txt")   # a long ask's whole text


def keep_whole(repo, aid, whole):
    """After the row is saved, so a failed save leaves no file for the next row with this id to inherit."""
    if len(whole) <= EXCERPT: return
    os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(full_path(repo, aid)), exist_ok=True)
    with open(full_path(repo, aid), "w") as f: f.write(whole + "\n")


class Lock:
    def __init__(self, repo):
        os.makedirs(SCOPE, exist_ok=True)
        self.f = open(os.path.join(SCOPE, repo + ".lock"), "w")
    def __enter__(self): fcntl.flock(self.f, fcntl.LOCK_EX); return self
    def __exit__(self, *a): fcntl.flock(self.f, fcntl.LOCK_UN); self.f.close()


def load(repo):
    try:
        with open(path(repo)) as f: return json.load(f)
    except (OSError, ValueError): return {"repo": repo, "asks": {}}


def save(repo, d):
    os.makedirs(SCOPE, exist_ok=True)
    d["updated"] = now()
    tmp = path(repo) + ".tmp"
    with open(tmp, "w") as f: json.dump(d, f, indent=2)
    os.replace(tmp, path(repo))


def repos():
    try: return sorted(f[:-5] for f in os.listdir(SCOPE) if f.endswith(".json"))
    except OSError: return []


# ---- the directives inside an ask ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deterministic, no LLM. A compound ask is where scope goes missing, so a row keeps the separate
# directive sentences beside its text and `show` prints them.
_ACKS = (r"thanks?|thx|ty|ok(?:ay)?|k|cool|nice|great|good|sure|yes|yeah|yep|no|nope|perfect|lgtm|"
         r"got ?it|right|agreed|👍|👌|🙏|🎉|❤️")
# AN ACKNOWLEDGEMENT CAN OPEN A REAL INSTRUCTION: "ok well do whatever you need to speed the landing up"
# is one. The lead is stripped and what follows is judged on its own; what is KEPT is still the whole
# sentence, ack and all. A bare ack strips to nothing and carries no cue of its own.
ACK_LEAD = re.compile(r"^\W*(?:%s)\b[\s,.:;!—–-]*" % _ACKS, re.I)
CUE_START = re.compile(r"^\W*(add|allow|always|answer|archive|ask|attach|be|break|build|cancel|change|"
                       r"check|clean|close|come|connect|cut|delete|deny|deploy|describe|disable|do|don'?t|"
                       r"drop|enable|ensure|explain|fix|fold|give|go|group|handle|have|hide|hold|install|"
                       r"keep|kill|label|leave|let|link|list|log|look|make|mark|merge|move|name|never|open|"
                       r"ping|post|prefer|print|pull|push|put|react|read|reduce|remind|remove|rename|reply|"
                       r"report|restart|review|rewrite|run|say|send|set|share|show|sort|split|start|stop|"
                       r"switch|take|tell|test|track|try|turn|update|upload|use|verify|wait|write)\b", re.I)
CUE_ANY = re.compile(r"\b(should|shouldn'?t|must|from now on|going forward|i want|i'?d like|i would like|"
                     r"can you|could you|would you|please|needs? to|has to|have to|make sure|instead of|"
                     r"stop \w+ing|no longer|(is|are|was|were|does|do)n'?t work(ing)?|not work(ing)?|i don'?t like|"
                     r"i do not like|not intuitive|rather than)\b", re.I)
SPLIT = re.compile(r"(?<=[.!?;])\s+|\n+")


def without_ack(s, rounds=3):
    """Strip the acknowledgements a sentence leads with: `ok, cool, do X` → `do X`. Bounded, so a sentence
    that is nothing but acks strips down to nothing and carries no cue of its own."""
    for _ in range(rounds):
        t = ACK_LEAD.sub("", s, count=1).strip()
        if t == s: break
        s = t
    return s


def parts_of(text):
    """The directive sentences inside a message. A compound ask is where scope goes missing, so they are
    kept separately and shown by `show` — the row is the message, the parts are what it actually asked."""
    out = []
    for s in SPLIT.split(text):
        s = s.strip(" -*·\t")
        if len(s.split()) < 2: continue
        core = without_ack(s)
        # CUE_START on what is left once the ack is off; CUE_ANY on the WHOLE sentence, because a cue can
        # begin with an ack word ("no longer …") and stripping it would lose one.
        if core and (CUE_START.match(core) or CUE_ANY.search(s)): out.append(s[:200])
    return out


def norm(text):
    return re.sub(r"[^a-z0-9 ]", "", re.sub(r"\s+", " ", text.lower())).strip()


def fingerprint(text): return hashlib.sha1(norm(text).encode()).hexdigest()[:12]


# ---- the ledger ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
def add(repo, text, who="owner", src="", track="", done_when="", check="", at="", replay=False):
    """Returns (id, repeated). The same ask made twice does not open a second row — it bumps `repeats`,
    which is the signal that matters: 'asked twice, no answer yet' is a line from a real handoff. Asked
    again after it was DECLINED, it goes back on the queue (the reason stays); asked again after it was
    VERIFIED it is a new row, because that is a regression, not a repeat. `replay` (import) matches every
    status and changes nothing — re-running a seed file must be a no-op, not a second ledger."""
    text = " ".join(text.split())
    if not text: return None, False
    fp = fingerprint(text)   # of the whole ask, so two long asks that part after the excerpt stay two rows
    whole, chars = text, len(text)
    if chars > EXCERPT:
        text = text[:EXCERPT].rstrip() + "…"
    with Lock(repo):
        d = load(repo)
        for a in d["asks"].values():
            if a["fp"] != fp: continue
            if replay: return a["id"], True
            if a["status"] in DONE and settled(a): continue
            a["repeats"] = a.get("repeats", 1) + 1
            a.setdefault("seen", []).append({"ts": at or now(), "src": src})
            if a["status"] == "declined":
                a["status"] = "open"; a["closed"] = ""
                a["notes"].append({"ts": now(), "msg": "asked again after it was declined — back on the "
                                                       "queue, with the reason it was declined kept above"})
            save(repo, d)
            keep_whole(repo, a["id"], whole)   # a row from before the side file gets its whole text now
            return a["id"], True
        n = 1
        while "a%d" % n in d["asks"]: n += 1
        aid = "a%d" % n
        d["asks"][aid] = {"id": aid, "text": text, "parts": parts_of(text), "fp": fp, "who": who,
                          "src": src, "track": track, "done_when": done_when, "status": "open",
                          "asked": at or now(), "repeats": 1, "evidence": "", "reason": "", "closed": "",
                          "check": check.strip(), "check_files": check_files(check), "checked": {}, "notes": []}
        if chars > EXCERPT:   # beside the ledger, so an ask with no --src (or a source that goes away) keeps its rest
            d["asks"][aid]["chars"] = chars
        save(repo, d)
        keep_whole(repo, aid, whole)
        return aid, False


# ---- the verification: a command, not a sentence ---------------------------------------------------
DEV = os.path.join(HOME, "dev")   # where a repo's checkout lives; the selfcheck points this at a scratch dir


def repo_dir(repo):
    """Where a row's check runs. The repo's own checkout if there is one, so a check can say `test -x bin/x`;
    otherwise the home directory, which is where a deployed thing actually lives."""
    p = os.path.join(DEV, repo)
    return p if os.path.isdir(p) else HOME


def has_check(a): return bool((a.get("check") or "").strip())


def passed(a): return (a.get("checked") or {}).get("rc") == 0


def cannot_run(a):
    """The check did not answer. Everything above this line saw one rc≠0 and called it "the check has not
    passed", which is how a69 read as a red boundary check for five days after its script went with its
    track's state dir. No answer is not the same as the answer no — but "no answer" has more than one cause
    (see shell_could_not_exec below), and this only tells CANNOT_RUN apart from a real failure, not one
    CANNOT_RUN cause from another."""
    c = a.get("checked") or {}
    return c.get("rc") in CANNOT_RUN or bool(c.get("gone"))


# bash's OWN diagnostic when it never found anything to run: always starts the line "bash: " and ends in one
# of its three fixed reasons. A check can return the SAME rc on purpose, though — a69's rebuilt boundary check
# exits 126 itself to say some of what it would measure never started (2026-09-07, no member/credential words
# here on purpose: this file must not learn what any one check means). The interpreter ran that check fine, so
# this text never appears in its output, and telling the reader to "give it a command that is there" would be
# advice for a problem it does not have. Generic and mechanical: it reads bash's own wording, not the check's.
_SHELL_EXEC_FAILURE = re.compile(r"^bash: .*(?:No such file or directory|Permission denied|command not found)",
                                 re.M)


def shell_could_not_exec(output):
    return bool(_SHELL_EXEC_FAILURE.search(output or ""))


def check_dir():
    d = os.path.join(SCOPE, CHECKS)
    os.makedirs(d, exist_ok=True)
    return d


def refuse_doomed(repo, aid, c):
    """A check is read months after it is written, long after the track that wrote it was cleaned up. This
    refusal used to live inside the check-cmd branch alone, which left `add --check` as a documented door to
    exactly the row it exists to prevent (review of #297)."""
    m = DOOMED.search(c or "")
    if m:
        sys.exit("cc-scope: that check RUNS a script out of %s, which does not outlive the track that "
                 "made it.\n  A track's state dir, its worktree and /tmp are all temporary by design, "
                 "and when one goes\n  the row reads as a failing check when in fact nothing has been "
                 "measured (a69, 2026-09-02 to 09-07).\n  Keep the script beside the ledger instead:\n"
                 "    cc-scope check-cmd %s %s --script %s" % (m.group(1), repo, aid, m.group(1)))


def install_script(repo, aid, src):
    """Copy a check script in beside the LEDGER. A track's state dir and its worktree are both temporary by
    design — the janitor takes them when the track is done — so a check that lives in one is a check with an
    expiry date on it. This is the durable place: the ledger's own dir, keyed by repo and ask. `--script` may
    name the installed file itself, to register what is there now; copying a file onto itself would fail."""
    src = os.path.expanduser(src)
    if not os.path.isfile(src):
        sys.exit("cc-scope: --script %s is not a file. Point it at the script as it is now: check-cmd copies "
                 "it in, so the original may be in a track dir that later goes away." % src)
    dst = os.path.join(check_dir(), "%s.%s.sh" % (repo, aid))
    if os.path.realpath(src) != os.path.realpath(dst):
        shutil.copyfile(src, dst)
    os.chmod(dst, 0o755)
    return dst


def check_files(c):
    """{path: sha} of every file under checks/ the command names, "" for one not there. Recorded on the row
    when the check is registered, and compared by `deliver`: see A CHECK IS NOT REPLACED IN SILENCE."""
    d = os.path.realpath(os.path.join(SCOPE, CHECKS))
    out = {}
    for tok in re.findall(r"[^\s;&|()<>`'\"=]+", c or ""):
        tok = re.sub(r"^(?:~|\$HOME|\$\{HOME\})(?=/)", lambda m: HOME, tok)
        p = os.path.realpath(tok)
        if tok.startswith("/") and p.startswith(d + os.sep):
            try:
                with open(p, "rb") as f: out[p] = hashlib.sha256(f.read()).hexdigest()[:16]
            except OSError: out[p] = ""
    return out


def drifted(a):
    """The scripts under checks/ this row's check runs that are not what was registered. A row registered
    before fingerprints has none recorded, and nothing tells its script from an edited one, so it counts.
    A script that is gone is not drift: it fails to run."""
    was = a.get("check_files")
    return [p for p, s in check_files(a.get("check")).items() if s and (was is None or was.get(p) != s)]


def check_text(a):
    """The row's check as the next seat has to read it: the command, then each script under checks/ it runs."""
    out, ed = ["    " + a["check"]], drifted(a)
    for p in check_files(a["check"]):
        try:
            body = open(p, errors="replace").read().splitlines()
        except OSError:
            out.append("    --- %s: not there" % p); continue
        out.append("    --- %s%s" % (p, "  (not what check-cmd fingerprinted)" if p in ed else ""))
        out += ["    | " + l for l in body[:40]] + (["    | ... %d more lines" % (len(body) - 40)] if len(body) > 40 else [])
    return "\n".join(out)


def settled(a):
    """Off the queue. `delivered` counts only if a check ACTUALLY PASSED. Every row this code delivers has
    one by construction — but rows written before it exists sit in `delivered` on sentences like "merged as
    PR #64 on main; deployed", which is the precise thing that is no longer evidence. Promoting those to
    settled because the word matches would close five real asks nobody ever measured, which is the failure
    this whole file is here to stop, committed by the fix for it. They go back on the queue instead."""
    if a.get("status") == "delivered":
        return passed(a)
    return a.get("status") in SETTLED


def record_undelivered(repo, a, rc, out):
    """A MERGED FIX THAT STILL FAILS ITS CHECK IS A FAILURE, and the check runner that saw it writes it to the failures
    ledger (docs/design-failure-noticing-2026-09-11.md, phase 2): kind delivery/merged-not-delivered, through
    `cc-green red`, ONCE per ask and merge — the key carries the merge's evidence, so the next tick on the same
    still-red ask writes nothing (`already`) and a new merge that still fails is a new record. Seven such rows were
    live on 2026-09-11 and nothing counted them. Never a verdict: the row's state and this command's exit code were
    decided before this ran, and a ledger that cannot be written is one line on stderr, not an error."""
    try:
        import tempfile
        key = "scope-deliver:%s:%s:%s" % (repo, a["id"], hashlib.sha1((a.get("evidence") or "").encode()).hexdigest()[:8])
        with tempfile.NamedTemporaryFile("w", prefix="cc-scope-check.", suffix=".out", delete=False) as f:
            f.write(out or ""); log = f.name
        try:
            r = subprocess.run([os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__)), "cc-green"), "red", repo,
                                "delivery/merged-not-delivered", key,
                                "--observed", ("ask %s of %s is merged (%s) and its check still fails (rc %d): %s \u2014 %s"
                                               % (a["id"], repo, a.get("evidence") or "no evidence", rc, a["check"],
                                                  " ".join((out or "").split())[-200:] or "(no output)"))[:600],
                                "--expected", "a merged fix passes the check its ask names", "--log", log,
                                "--writer", "cc-scope", "--link", "ask=%s" % a["id"], "--link", "check=%s" % a["check"][:200]]
                               + (["--link", "track=%s" % a["track"]] if a.get("track") else []),
                               capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
            word = ((r.stdout.strip() or r.stderr.strip()).splitlines() or ["not recorded: cc-green red said nothing"])[-1]
        finally:
            try: os.unlink(log)
            except OSError: pass
    except Exception as e:   # the ledger never decides a delivery
        word = "not recorded: %s: %s" % (e.__class__.__name__, e)
    print("cc-scope: failures ledger (%s): %s" % (repo, word), file=sys.stderr)


def run_check(repo, a):
    """(rc, output). The EXIT CODE is the whole answer — a check prints for the human reading the failure,
    and decides nothing by what it prints. Anything that goes wrong running it is a failure, not a pass:
    the one direction this must never err in is towards 'delivered'. Failure is not one thing, though:
    126/127 mean the command was never there to answer (CANNOT_RUN), and a hang means it ran and would not
    finish, so a timeout comes back as its own code rather than borrowing the one for 'not found'."""
    env = dict(os.environ)
    # A check names the box's own tools the way a person would at a prompt (`cc-limit selfcheck`), so they have to
    # BE on PATH when it runs. The landing queue's worker has a PATH without the user's bin dir, and three checks
    # in a row died there with rc=127 while the same command passed by hand (2026-09-02) — a tooling failure read
    # as "not delivered". The dir this file lives in is that bin dir, so prepend it and let a check stay readable.
    # os.defpath is the floor: passing env= makes subprocess resolve the INTERPRETER through this PATH too, so an
    # inherited PATH that is empty or broken would lose `bash` itself and every check would read as rc=127.
    env["PATH"] = os.pathsep.join([os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__)), env.get("PATH", "") or os.defpath])
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(["bash", "-c", a["check"]], cwd=repo_dir(repo), capture_output=True, text=True,
                           timeout=CHECK_TIMEOUT, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, env=env)
        return r.returncode, r.stdout + r.stderr
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        return CHECK_TIMED_OUT, "the check did not finish inside %ds" % CHECK_TIMEOUT
    except Exception as e:
        return 127, "%s: %s" % (e.__class__.__name__, e)


# ---- a check that reads what the tree no longer has ------------------------------------------------
# The header's third cause of no answer. What a check names is read the way a person would: a bare cc-<name> is
# a tool of this box (core/bin/ or bin/, the two places they have lived), a path whose first segment is a
# directory of the checkout is a file of the repo, and ~/.cc/state/<name>/ is the ledger cc-<name> keeps. Only
# the checkout's own base branch is asked (origin/HEAD as last fetched, else HEAD) and only git's own record of a
# deletion counts: a name git never had is not gone, it was never there, and that is the check's own business.
TOOL_DIRS = ("core/bin", "bin")
TOOL_NAME = re.compile(r"cc-[a-z][a-z0-9-]*")
STATE_OF = re.compile(_HOMEISH + r"/\.cc/state/([A-Za-z0-9_-]+)(?=/)")


def git_out(root, *args):
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", root, *args], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=20)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return ""
    return r.stdout.strip() if r.returncode == 0 else ""


def names_gone(repo, a):
    """['<what> — deleted by <sha> <date> <subject>'] for each thing this row's check (and the scripts under
    checks/ it runs) names that the repo's base branch no longer has. [] when ~/dev/<repo> is no git checkout:
    a check can only be read against a tree that is there."""
    root = repo_dir(repo)
    if not git_out(root, "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel"):
        return []
    ref = (git_out(root, "symbolic-ref", "-q", "--short", "refs/remotes/origin/HEAD")
           or ("origin/main" if git_out(root, "rev-parse", "--verify", "-q", "origin/main") else "HEAD"))
    have = set()
    for f in git_out(root, "ls-tree", "-r", "--name-only", ref).splitlines():
        parts = f.split("/")
        have.update("/".join(parts[:i]) for i in range(1, len(parts) + 1))
    text = a.get("check") or ""
    for p in check_files(text):
        try: text += "\n" + open(p, errors="replace").read()
        except OSError: pass
    want = {}     # what the check names -> (the paths git would have had for it, how the line reads)
    for tok in re.findall(r"[^\s;&|()<>`'\"=,]+", text):
        tok = tok.rstrip(".:/")
        if TOOL_NAME.fullmatch(tok):
            want[tok] = ([d + "/" + tok for d in TOOL_DIRS], tok)
        elif ("/" in tok and not tok.startswith(("/", "~", "$", ".")) and ".." not in tok.split("/")
              and os.path.isdir(os.path.join(root, tok.split("/")[0]))):
            want[tok] = ([tok], tok)
    for m in STATE_OF.finditer(text):
        want.setdefault(m.group(0), ([d + "/cc-" + m.group(1) for d in TOOL_DIRS], "%s (the ledger cc-%s kept)" % (m.group(0), m.group(1))))
    out = []
    for paths, label in want.values():
        if any(p in have for p in paths):
            continue
        d = git_out(root, "log", "-1", "--diff-filter=D", "--format=%h %cs %s", ref, "--", *paths)
        if d:
            sha, date, subj = (d.split(" ", 2) + ["", ""])[:3]
            out.append("%s \u2014 deleted by %s %s %s" % (label, sha, date, subj[:60] + ("\u2026" if len(subj) > 60 else "")))
    return out


def get(repo, aid):
    a = load(repo)["asks"].get(aid)
    if not a: sys.exit("cc-scope: %s has no ask '%s'" % (repo, aid))
    return a


def edit(repo, aid, fn):
    with Lock(repo):
        d = load(repo)
        if aid not in d["asks"]: sys.exit("cc-scope: %s has no ask '%s'" % (repo, aid))
        fn(d["asks"][aid]); save(repo, d)


def select(repo, status=None, track=None, everything=False):
    """No status named = the unsettled ones: what is still owed. A declined ask is settled — it is a decision
    already taken, so it stops being work — but `--all` and `--status declined` still show it, with its reason."""
    asks = sorted(load(repo)["asks"].values(), key=lambda a: a["asked"])
    if not everything:
        asks = [a for a in asks if (a["status"] == status if status else not settled(a))]
    if track: asks = [a for a in asks if a.get("track") == track]
    return asks


def line(repo, a, many=False):
    flag = " ⚠ asked %d×" % a["repeats"] if a.get("repeats", 1) > 1 else ""
    tail = " [%s]" % a["track"] if a.get("track") else ""
    head = "%s/" % repo if many else ""
    why = "\n       ⊘ %s" % a["reason"] if a["status"] == "declined" and a.get("reason") else ""
    # A merged row is the one the ledger used to call done. It says so on the line you actually read, because
    # "has home been updated yet?" is the question this is here to answer without anyone having to go and look.
    if a["status"] == "merged":
        why = "\n       ⚠ merged, NOT delivered — %s" % (
            "its check reads what the tree no longer has (%s), so nothing has been measured — not a red check: %s"
            % ("; ".join(a["checked"]["gone"]), a["check"]) if (a.get("checked") or {}).get("gone") else
            "its check CANNOT RUN, so nothing has been measured either way — not a red check: %s" % a["check"]
            if cannot_run(a) else "its check has not passed: %s" % a["check"] if has_check(a)
            else "it names no check, so nothing has observed it")
    elif a["status"] == "delivered" and not passed(a):
        why = "\n       ⚠ NOT delivered — closed on a sentence before a check was required, so it is back on the queue"
    return "%s %s%-4s %s%s%s%s" % (MARK.get(a["status"], "?"), head, a["id"], a["text"][:96], tail, flag, why)


# ---- the hook that is no longer one -----------------------------------------------------------------
def hook():
    """A NO-OP. The live ~/.claude/settings.json may still name `cc-scope hook` as a UserPromptSubmit hook:
    claude-managed.json retires it, so `cc-settings check` names it stale and `cc-settings apply` removes it, but
    until the owner applies that a missing subcommand would put usage text on stderr into every session, on
    every prompt. A fresh box never registers it. Drain stdin, print nothing, exit 0."""
    try: sys.stdin.read()
    except Exception: pass


# ---- commands --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
def opt(args, name, default=""):
    if name in args:
        i = args.index(name)
        v = args[i + 1] if len(args) > i + 1 else ""
        del args[i:i + 2]
        return v
    return default


def flag(args, name):
    if name in args: args.remove(name); return True
    return False


def main(argv):
    if not argv or argv[0] in ("-h", "--help"): sys.exit(__doc__)
    cmd, args = argv[0], list(argv[1:])
    js = flag(args, "--json")

    if cmd == "add":
        repo, text = args[0], args[1]
        # ONE read of --check, reused: opt() DELETES the option and its value out of args, so asking twice
        # hands the second caller "" and the row silently stores no check at all (review of #297).
        chk = opt(args, "--check")
        refuse_doomed(repo, "<new>", chk)   # the same door as check-cmd, and it was open here
        aid, rep = add(repo, text, who=opt(args, "--who", "owner"), src=opt(args, "--src"),
                       track=opt(args, "--track"), done_when=opt(args, "--done-when"),
                       check=chk, at=opt(args, "--at"))
        print("%s %s%s" % (repo, aid, " (asked again)" if rep else ""))


    elif cmd == "list":
        status, track, everything = opt(args, "--status") or None, opt(args, "--track") or None, flag(args, "--all")
        rs = [args[0]] if args else repos()
        rows = [(r, a) for r in rs for a in select(r, status, track, everything)]
        if js: print(json.dumps([a for _, a in rows], indent=2)); return
        if not rows: print("no asks" if everything else "nothing %s" % (status or "unsettled")); return
        for r, a in rows: print(line(r, a, many=len(rs) > 1))

    elif cmd == "show":
        a = get(args[0], args[1])
        print("%s %s [%s]%s" % (args[0], a["id"], a["status"],
                                "  ⚠ asked %d×" % a["repeats"] if a.get("repeats", 1) > 1 else ""))
        print("  asked   %s by %s%s" % (a["asked"], a["who"], "  (%s)" % a["src"] if a["src"] else ""))
        if a.get("track"):     print("  track   %s" % a["track"])
        if a.get("done_when"): print("  done when %s" % a["done_when"])
        if a.get("reason"):    print("  declined because %s" % a["reason"])
        if has_check(a):
            # The check and what it last DID, on the same two lines: the notes below carry every run, and
            # "has this been measured at all?" should not need reading them in order.
            c = a.get("checked") or {}
            print("  check   %s" % a["check"])
            print("          %s" % ("never run" if not c else
                                    "passed %s" % c["ts"] if c.get("rc") == 0 else
                                    "COULD NOT RUN %s (rc %d) — nothing measured" % (c["ts"], c["rc"])
                                    if c.get("rc") in CANNOT_RUN else
                                    "reads what the tree no longer has %s (rc %d) — nothing measured: %s"
                                    % (c["ts"], c["rc"], "; ".join(c["gone"])) if c.get("gone") else
                                    "ran and FAILED %s (rc %d)" % (c["ts"], c["rc"])))
        print("  %s" % a["text"])
        if a.get("chars"):
            side = full_path(args[0], a["id"])
            print("  (the first %d of %d characters; %s, from %s)"
                  % (EXCERPT, a["chars"], "the whole ask is in " + side if os.path.exists(side) else
                     "no side file, because the row predates it", a["src"] or "no source given"))
        for p in a.get("parts", []): print("    – %s" % p)
        for s in a.get("seen", []): print("  again   %s %s" % (s["ts"], s.get("src", "") or "-"))
        if a["evidence"]: print("  evidence %s%s" % (a["evidence"], "  (%s)" % a["closed"] if a["closed"] else ""))
        for n in a["notes"]: print("  note    %s %s" % (n["ts"], n["msg"]))

    elif cmd == "check-cmd":
        script, why = opt(args, "--script"), opt(args, "--replace").strip()
        repo, aid = args[0], args[1]
        a = get(repo, aid)     # before anything is copied anywhere
        if has_check(a) and not why:
            sys.exit("cc-scope: %s %s already has a check, and check-cmd does not replace one in silence (see A "
                     "CHECK IS NOT\n  REPLACED IN SILENCE in --help). Read the one that is there:\n%s\n"
                     "  Replace it only if it is wrong, and say why; the reason is kept on the row:\n"
                     "    cc-scope check-cmd %s %s ... --replace \"<why the check above is wrong>\""
                     % (repo, aid, check_text(a), repo, aid))
        if script:
            c = "bash %s" % install_script(repo, aid, script)
        else:
            c = (args[2] if len(args) > 2 else "").strip()
            if not c: sys.exit("cc-scope: check-cmd needs a command — one that exits 0 only when the ask is TRUE")
            refuse_doomed(repo, aid, c)
        def upd(x):
            if has_check(x):
                x["notes"].append({"ts": now(), "msg": "check replaced (was: %s) because: %s" % (x["check"], why)})
            x.update(check=c, checked={}, check_files=check_files(c))
        edit(repo, aid, upd)
        print("%s %s check: %s" % (repo, aid, c))

    elif cmd == "deliver":
        # THE HOLE THIS CLOSES: this used to take `--evidence "merged as PR #N"` and believe it. It now runs
        # the row's own check and believes only the exit code. There is no argument that can talk it round.
        repo, aid = args[0], args[1]
        track, ev = opt(args, "--track"), opt(args, "--evidence")
        a = get(repo, aid)
        if track: edit(repo, aid, lambda x: x.update(track=track))
        if not has_check(a):
            print("cc-scope: %s %s names no check — UNVERIFIED. It stays '%s' and it stays on the queue: a merge\n"
                  "  is not delivery, and nothing here has observed this reach anyone. Give it one —\n"
                  "    cc-scope check-cmd %s %s '<command that exits 0 only when the ask is true>'\n"
                  "  (%s)\n"
                  "  or, if no mechanical check can exist, put your own name on it and say what you looked at:\n"
                  "    cc-scope verify %s %s --evidence \"...\"" % (repo, aid, a["status"], repo, aid, EXAMPLES,
                                                                   repo, aid),
                  file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        if drifted(a):
            print("cc-scope: %s %s is NOT delivered and its check was NOT run: a script it runs is not what\n"
                  "  check-cmd fingerprinted. It was edited in place, or registered before scripts were\n"
                  "  fingerprinted. Read it:\n%s\n"
                  "  If it is right, register it as it is now, and say why:\n"
                  "    cc-scope check-cmd %s %s %s --replace \"<why>\"" % (repo, aid, check_text(a), repo, aid,
                                                                      shlex.quote(a["check"])),
                  file=sys.stderr)
            return 3
        rc, o = run_check(repo, a)
        tail = " ".join((o or "").split())[-300:]
        # A FAILING CHECK IS READ ONCE MORE before it is called a failure: one that names what the tree no longer
        # has measured nothing (the header's third cause). A pass is never second-guessed.
        dead = names_gone(repo, a) if rc and rc not in CANNOT_RUN else []
        silent = rc in CANNOT_RUN or bool(dead)     # no answer, which is not the answer no
        def upd(x):
            rec = {"ts": now(), "cmd": x["check"], "rc": rc, "out": tail}
            if dead: rec["gone"] = dead
            if rc == 0:
                x["checked"] = rec
                x["status"], x["closed"] = "delivered", now()
                x["evidence"] = ((ev + " \u00b7 ") if ev else "") + "check passed: " + x["check"]
            elif silent:
                x["notes"].append({"ts": now(), "msg": ("check reads what the tree no longer has (rc %d) %s \u2014 %s"
                                                       % (rc, x["check"], "; ".join(dead)[:200])) if dead else
                                                      "check COULD NOT RUN (rc %d) %s \u2014 %s" % (rc, x["check"], tail[:160])})
                # A check that was never asked must not erase the answer of one that was: overwriting a green
                # record with rc=127 would drag a delivered row back onto the queue because a FILE went away.
                if not passed(x): x["checked"] = rec
            else:
                x["checked"] = rec
                x["notes"].append({"ts": now(), "msg": "check FAILED (rc %d) %s \u2014 %s" % (rc, x["check"], tail[:160])})
        edit(repo, aid, upd)
        if rc and not silent and a.get("status") == "merged":
            record_undelivered(repo, a, rc, o)   # "a merged fix still fails its check": the ledger's line, once per merge
        if dead:
            print("cc-scope: %s %s \u2014 NO ANSWER: its check reads what the tree no longer has, so its rc %d measures\n"
                  "  nothing%s. This is NOT a failed check.\n  %s\n%s\n"
                  "  Scope the ask against what is there now (`git show <sha> --stat` says where it went) and give it a\n"
                  "  check that can be true, or decline it:\n"
                  "    cc-scope check-cmd %s %s '<command>' --replace \"<why>\"  \u00b7  cc-scope decline %s %s --why \"<why>\""
                  % (repo, aid, rc, " and the row stays '%s' on the run that did pass" % a["status"] if passed(a) else "",
                     a["check"], "\n".join("    " + d for d in dead), repo, aid, repo, aid), file=sys.stderr)
            return 3
        if silent:
            if shell_could_not_exec(o):
                # bash itself never found anything to run — the vanished-script cause #297 was written for.
                fix = ("  Give it a command that is there, or keep the script beside the ledger where it "
                       "outlives\n  the track that wrote it:  cc-scope check-cmd %s %s --script <file> --replace \"<why>\""
                       % (repo, aid))
            else:
                # The interpreter ran the check fine; the check chose this rc itself to say it could not
                # measure something. That is not a missing command, so this must not send the reader to fix
                # a path that was never broken — the reason is whatever it printed above, on the check.
                fix = ("  That is not a missing command \u2014 read what it printed above for the reason, and "
                       "rerun\n  once it changes:  cc-scope deliver %s %s" % (repo, aid))
            print("cc-scope: %s %s \u2014 its check COULD NOT RUN (rc %d). This is NOT a failed check: nothing was\n"
                  "  measured, so the ask has not been answered either way%s.\n  %s\n  %s\n%s"
                  % (repo, aid, rc, " and the row stays '%s' on the run that did pass" % a["status"]
                     if passed(a) else "", a["check"], tail[:200] or "(no output)", fix),
                  file=sys.stderr)
            return 3
        if rc:
            print("cc-scope: %s %s is NOT delivered — its check failed (rc %d), so it stays '%s' and stays open.\n"
                  "  %s\n  %s" % (repo, aid, rc, a["status"], a["check"], tail[:200] or "(no output)"),
                  file=sys.stderr)
            return 1
        print("%s %s delivered — its check passed: %s" % (repo, aid, a["check"]))

    elif cmd in ("claim", "merged", "verify", "declined", "decline", "done", "drop"):
        state = {"claim": "claimed", "merged": "merged", "verify": "verified", "done": "verified",
                 "decline": "declined", "declined": "declined", "drop": "declined"}[cmd]
        repo, aid = args[0], args[1]
        track, ev, why = opt(args, "--track"), opt(args, "--evidence"), opt(args, "--why")
        a = load(repo)["asks"].get(aid, {})
        if state == "merged":
            if not ev:
                sys.exit("cc-scope: merged needs --evidence — the PR or commit that landed")
            if a.get("status") in DONE:   # a re-run must never drag a delivered row backwards
                print("%s %s is already %s — a re-merge does not undo it" % (repo, aid, a["status"])); return 0
        if state == "verified":
            # A person may close what no machine can measure. They may NOT overrule a check they themselves wrote:
            # a red check with a confident sentence beside it is the pair that closed two rows the owner then reopened.
            if has_check(a) and not passed(a):
                sys.exit("cc-scope: %s names a check and it has not passed — the ledger runs the check, it does "
                         "not take a sentence for it.\n  %s\nRun it (`cc-scope deliver %s %s`), or replace it "
                         "with one that can pass (`cc-scope check-cmd %s %s ... --replace \"<why>\"`)."
                         % (aid, a["check"], repo, aid, repo, aid))
            if not (ev or a.get("evidence")):
                sys.exit("cc-scope: verify needs --evidence — name what YOU looked at, in your own words. This is "
                         "the by-hand path for an ask no command can measure, so a PR number is not an answer.")
        if state == "declined" and not why:   # the reason IS the decision: without one this is a drop, and a drop
            sys.exit("cc-scope: decline needs --why — a decline without a reason is the drop this ledger exists "
                     "to catch. Say, in your own words, why it should not be built.")
        def upd(a):
            a["status"] = state
            if track: a["track"] = track
            if ev: a["evidence"] = ev
            if why: a["reason"] = why
            a["closed"] = now() if state in SETTLED else ""
        edit(repo, aid, upd)
        print("%s %s %s%s" % (repo, aid, state,
              " — NOT delivered: nothing has observed this yet" if state == "merged" else ""))

    elif cmd == "unverified":
        # The honest column. Every row the box has said landed, where no check has ever gone green: the merged
        # ones still owed, and the ones a person closed on their own word. Both are real; neither was measured.
        rs = [args[0]] if args else repos()
        rows = [(r, a) for r in rs for a in select(r, None, opt(args, "--track") or None, everything=True)
                if a["status"] in ("merged", "delivered", "verified") and not passed(a)]
        if js: print(json.dumps([a for _, a in rows], indent=2)); return 1 if rows else 0
        if not rows:
            print("cc-scope: nothing unverified — everything the box says landed was checked, and it passed")
            return 0
        print("cc-scope: %d ask(s) the box says landed with nothing observed at the other end:" % len(rows))
        for r, a in rows:
            if a["status"] == "merged":
                why = ("STILL OPEN, names no check — cc-scope check-cmd %s %s '<cmd>'" % (r, a["id"])
                       if not has_check(a) else
                       "STILL OPEN and NEVER MEASURED — its check reads what the tree no longer has (%s), which is not a red check: %s"
                       % ("; ".join(a["checked"]["gone"]), a["check"]) if (a.get("checked") or {}).get("gone") else
                       "STILL OPEN and NEVER MEASURED — its check CANNOT RUN, which is not a red check: %s"
                       % a["check"] if cannot_run(a) else
                       "STILL OPEN, its check has not passed: %s" % a["check"])
            elif a["status"] == "delivered":   # written before a check was required — a sentence, not a measurement
                why = "STILL OPEN, closed on a sentence: %s" % (a.get("evidence") or "no evidence")
            else:   # verified: a person's own word, which is allowed and is not the same as measured
                why = "closed by hand, never measured: %s" % (a.get("evidence") or "no evidence")
            print("  %-4s %s%s" % (a["id"], "%s/" % r if len(rs) > 1 else "", a["text"][:88]))
            print("       %s" % why)
        return 1

    elif cmd == "track":
        edit(args[0], args[1], lambda a: a.update(track=args[2])); print("%s %s → %s" % tuple(args[:3]))

    elif cmd == "note":
        edit(args[0], args[1], lambda a: a["notes"].append({"ts": now(), "msg": args[2]}))
        print("%s %s noted" % (args[0], args[1]))

    elif cmd == "check":          # 'done' measured against the asks: exit 1 while anything is still open
        repo = args[0]; track = opt(args, "--track") or None
        open_ = select(repo, None, track)
        where = " for %s" % track if track else ""
        if not open_: print("cc-scope: nothing unsettled%s" % where); return 0
        m = sum(1 for a in open_ if a["status"] == "merged")
        print("cc-scope: %d unsettled ask(s)%s%s. Each one is delivered by its OWN check passing "
              "(`cc-scope check-cmd %s <id> \"<cmd>\"` then `cc-scope deliver %s <id>`), or closed by hand when "
              "no command can measure it (`cc-scope verify %s <id> --evidence \"what you looked at\"`), or "
              "declined with a reason (`cc-scope decline %s <id> --why \"…\"`). A PR number is not any of "
              "those — leaving it is the one thing that is not a decision:"
              % (len(open_), where, " — %d of them merged but NOT delivered" % m if m else "",
                 repo, repo, repo, repo))
        for a in open_: print("  " + line(repo, a))
        return 1

    elif cmd in ("count", "digest"):
        rs = [args[0]] if args else repos()
        n = sum(len(select(r)) for r in rs)
        again = sum(1 for r in rs for a in select(r) if a.get("repeats", 1) > 1)
        if cmd == "count": print(n); return
        # "merged but not delivered" is its own number because it is the owner's own question — he asked
        # "has it landed yet / what is not done yet" three times, and a single open count could not answer it.
        m = sum(1 for r in rs for a in select(r) if a["status"] == "merged")
        why = ", ".join(filter(None, ["%d merged but not delivered" % m if m else "",
                                      "%d asked more than once" % again if again else ""]))
        if n: print("• 📋 %d ask%s of yours still open%s" % (n, "" if n == 1 else "s",
                                                            " (%s)" % why if why else ""))

    elif cmd == "import":         # seed/replay: <at>TAB<src>TAB<track>TAB<status>TAB<evidence>TAB<text>
        repo, f = args[0], args[1]
        n = new = 0
        for raw in open(f):
            raw = raw.rstrip("\n")
            if not raw.strip() or raw.lstrip().startswith("#"): continue
            c = raw.split("\t")
            if len(c) < 6: sys.exit("cc-scope import: 6 tab-separated columns expected, got %d: %s" % (len(c), raw[:60]))
            at, src, track, status, ev, text = [x.strip() for x in c[:6]]
            aid, rep = add(repo, text, src=src, track=track, at=at, replay=True)
            n += 1; new += 0 if rep else 1
            if status and status != "open":
                if status not in STATES: sys.exit("cc-scope import: '%s' is not one of %s" % (status, "/".join(STATES)))
                edit(repo, aid, lambda a, s=status, e=ev: a.update(   # no `closed` on a backfill: when it was
                    status=s, **({"reason": e} if s == "declined" else {"evidence": e})))   # settled is not recorded
        print("cc-scope: imported %d ask(s) into %s (%d new)" % (n, repo, new))

    elif cmd == "hook":
        hook()

    elif cmd == "selfcheck":
        return selfcheck()

    else:
        sys.exit(__doc__)
    return 0


def selfcheck(*a, **k):
    """The cases are core/tests/selfcheck/cc-scope.py, run in THIS namespace so their stubs and `global`
    rebinds land on the tool as they did when the body was here. Defining them replaces this selfcheck()."""
    path = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__))), "tests", "selfcheck", "cc-scope.py")
    with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as fh:
        exec(compile(fh.read(), path, "exec"), globals())
    return globals()["selfcheck"](*a, **k)


def quiet(argv):
    """main() with its output swallowed — the selfcheck asserts on exit codes, not on the printed report.
    stderr too: the refusals below are meant to be long and explain themselves, and a passing suite should
    not scroll a page of advice for a case that behaved exactly as it was asserted to."""
    import io, contextlib
    buf = io.StringIO()
    with contextlib.redirect_stdout(buf), contextlib.redirect_stderr(buf): return main(argv) or 0


def run_capture(argv):
    import io, contextlib
    buf = io.StringIO()
    with contextlib.redirect_stdout(buf): main(argv)
    return buf.getvalue()


def run_capture_err(argv):
    """(exit code, stdout+stderr) — `deliver`'s CANNOT_RUN advice is written to stderr, and telling one cause
    of it from another means asserting on the words, not just on the row's state afterwards."""
    import io, contextlib
    buf = io.StringIO()
    with contextlib.redirect_stdout(buf), contextlib.redirect_stderr(buf): rc = main(argv) or 0
    return rc, buf.getvalue()


if __name__ == "__main__":
    try: sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]) or 0)
    except IndexError: sys.exit(__doc__)
    except BrokenPipeError: sys.exit(0)
